1. DIMACS file format
example.cnf

formula:
(x1 ∨ x3 ∨ ¬x4) ∧ (x4) ∧ (x2 ∨ ¬x3)

c Example CNF format file
c
p cnf 4 3
1 3 -4 0
4 0 2
-3

line starts with 'c' = comment
only one line starting with 'p' after comments = problem line
number 4 = number of variables, i.e. 1, 2, 3, 4
number 3 = number of clauses, delimited by 0
- = not

2. CNF = set of set because of;
Commutativity = order doesn't matter
OR: x ∨ y = y ∨ x

Associativity = grouping doesn't matter
x ∨ (y ∨ z) = (x ∨ y) ∨ z

Absorption = literal dominates over next clause if operators flipped
A ∨ (A ∧ B) = A
A ∧ (A ∨ B) = A

3. Resolution: negating literals are like antimatter, collapse them into a union
1 clause has literal x
other clause has its negation
resolvent = their union without that literal (and its negation)

4. CNF = {} = T
DNF = {} = ⊥

5. "Unit" resolution = resolution where first clause a literal
first clause vanishes, taking its negation from the second clause

6. Boolean Constraint Propagation
= unit resolve till the end
for x in unitResolved(X):
	F = F / clause.contains(x)
	for f in F:
		f = f / not x
// x resolved = assume I(x) must = T
// disjunctions with x = T resolve as T
// disjunctions with (not x = F) so ignore (not x)

7. "Pure" literal rule
for l in literal:
	l.isOnlyPositiveOrNegative
	F.removeWhere( f.contains(l) )
	// l/¬l = T is trivial assumption for proving SAT

8. DP
1. unit literal (del l, clauses with l, excise ¬l from clauses)
2. pure literal (wholesale remove clauses with l/¬l)
3. resolution (literal x as antimatter, union)

single empty clause {} derived = UNSAT, otherwise SAT
e.g. resolving (x),(¬x) derives empty clause => UNSAT
but resolving (x) derivesempty CNF => SAT

9. Case splitting
pick literal x
	case 1
		assume I(x) = T
			F = F/clause.contains(x)
			F.forEach( f -> f.clauses.forEach( c.remove(¬x) ) )
			empty clause = return false
			empty F = return true
	case 2
		assume I(¬x) = T
			F = F/clause.contains(¬x)
			F.forEach( f -> f.clauses.forEach( c.remove(x) ) )
			empty clause = return false
			empty F = return true

10. DPLL
1. unit literal rule
2. pure literal rule
3. case splitting (instead of naive resolution)

11. A Trail is a list of pairs
T = [ (l1, g/d) ... (ln, g/d) ]
ls are unique literals
guessed/deduced

12. iterativeDPLL():
	trail.push( firstGuess )
	loop:
		if literals.noneLeft:
			return SAT
		if !deducedLiteral:
			trail.push( guessedLiteral )
			continue
			
		trail.push( deducedLiteral )
		if !contradicts:
			continue

		if contradicts:
			trail = trail.backtracked()
			if trail.isEmpty: return UNSAT
			continue
	
		throw UnreachableCodeException()

13. backtrack():
	loop:
		if trail.isEmpty: return trail
		if trail.last.isDeduced:
			trail.pop()
			continue
		if trail.last.isGuessed:
			trail.last = trail.last.negated()
			trail.last = trail.last.flipTo( isDeduced )
			return trail

14. counter based data structures say
clause C
- counter nVariables
- counter nTrue
- counter nFalse
UNSAT = nVariables == nFalse
bypass = nTrue > 1
unitResolve = nVariables - nFalse = 1

